{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Snow Algae Burst: Microbial Life in a High-Arctic Meltwater Pond",
    "date": "2026-11-24",
    "lesson": "Each summer, shallow meltwater ponds pool on top of Arctic and Greenland sea ice and glacier surfaces. Sunlight floods these pools nearly around the clock, but the water is almost nutrient-free - a watery desert lit like a floodlight. Psychrophilic bacteria and small diatom chains that blow in on wind-borne dust survive by scavenging every trace of dissolved organic carbon they can find, while a resident photosynthetic bacterium banks what little carbon it can fix during the brief hours the sun dips low. The lesson: extreme light does not mean extreme productivity if nutrients are the bottleneck - and a population here lives or dies by how efficiently it searches, not how fast it can swim.",
    "citation": "Lutz, S. et al. (2017) The biogeography of red snow microbiomes and their role in melting arctic glaciers; Mueller, D.R. et al. supraglacial meltwater pond microbiology",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "High-Arctic supraglacial meltwater pond, summer continuous daylight, oligotrophic ice-surface microbial community"
  },
